1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:04,240
Sometimes in life there are lists that you do not want to change.

2
00:00:04,280 --> 00:00:07,880
Maybe it's a list of your favorite people.

3
00:00:07,880 --> 00:00:10,240
Maybe it's a list of your favorite restaurants.

4
00:00:10,280 --> 00:00:12,680
Maybe it's a list of your favorite movies.

5
00:00:12,680 --> 00:00:16,120
Whatever it may be, sometimes lists shouldn't change.

6
00:00:16,360 --> 00:00:19,360
Now, at the moment, you can see Alice can change very easily, and it's very handy.

7
00:00:19,360 --> 00:00:21,200
And there are lots of reasons why we want to do that.

8
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:25,320
But what if we don't want Alice to change regardless?

9
00:00:25,640 --> 00:00:32,480
Okay, in Python, that list that cannot change is called a tuple or tuple.

10
00:00:32,520 --> 00:00:33,920
People pronounce it differently.

11
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:37,120
I'm going to go with tuple tuple because it rhymes with couple.

12
00:00:37,160 --> 00:00:38,920
Yeah easier to remember.

13
00:00:39,160 --> 00:00:39,400
Okay.

14
00:00:39,480 --> 00:00:46,960
So this unchanging list is actually quite easy to change from a changing list.

15
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:55,240
So in order to make this a tuple, all I'm doing is I am putting in brackets instead of square brackets.

16
00:00:55,360 --> 00:00:56,920
Now what this means.

17
00:00:56,960 --> 00:01:00,340
This means that no matter what, I cannot change this list.

18
00:01:00,340 --> 00:01:01,780
I cannot add a name.

19
00:01:01,780 --> 00:01:03,940
I cannot change a name.

20
00:01:04,380 --> 00:01:05,180
Uh, it.

21
00:01:05,180 --> 00:01:07,420
Once it's created, it's a solid list.

22
00:01:07,460 --> 00:01:08,940
So those people are coming to the party.

23
00:01:08,940 --> 00:01:09,940
They're my best friends.

24
00:01:10,620 --> 00:01:12,380
So before where?

25
00:01:12,420 --> 00:01:18,980
This program is exactly the same as we had it before we changed this, uh, name, Sarah, to the name

26
00:01:18,980 --> 00:01:20,260
that the user inputted.

27
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:21,580
So now we're going to try that again.

28
00:01:21,580 --> 00:01:24,900
Run the same program, but now using a tuple.

29
00:01:25,460 --> 00:01:29,180
So let's re tapping clear.

30
00:01:29,380 --> 00:01:36,660
And straight away it's going to give you an error saying tuple object does not support item assignment.

31
00:01:36,660 --> 00:01:41,820
So what that means is basically in simple terms the list cannot be changed.

32
00:01:42,020 --> 00:01:43,380
Yeah that's what it means in English.

33
00:01:43,380 --> 00:01:48,020
Isn't it beautiful tuple object does not support item assignment in English.

34
00:01:48,020 --> 00:01:51,100
It means you can't change this list.

35
00:01:51,100 --> 00:01:54,300
Dude, it's a solid stuck list.

36
00:01:54,340 --> 00:01:59,670
The reason you would want to do this is basically to stop yourself from making silly mistakes.

37
00:01:59,670 --> 00:02:05,470
So if I had had this as a list with the square brackets, but I didn't want to change it, it was something

38
00:02:05,470 --> 00:02:06,270
that was important.

39
00:02:06,270 --> 00:02:09,110
It was something that stayed the same throughout the whole program.

40
00:02:09,270 --> 00:02:12,750
And somehow I made a silly mistake and I did get changed.

41
00:02:12,870 --> 00:02:17,510
And you run your program and suddenly it's not doing what you wanted to do, and you don't understand

42
00:02:17,510 --> 00:02:17,990
why.

43
00:02:18,030 --> 00:02:21,510
And the reason is because something was changed that wasn't meant to change.

44
00:02:21,670 --> 00:02:27,910
But if I had in the beginning defined it as being a tuple, then as soon as I try to change it somewhere

45
00:02:27,910 --> 00:02:29,790
later in the program, it wouldn't let me.

46
00:02:29,830 --> 00:02:33,110
It would throw that error saying, sorry, you can't change this.

47
00:02:33,150 --> 00:02:37,910
Classic example would be you wanted to create a list that had the the months of the year.

48
00:02:37,910 --> 00:02:39,590
So January, February, March, they don't change.

49
00:02:39,590 --> 00:02:44,950
They always the same months of the year that would be created rather than just as a list but as a tuple.

50
00:02:44,990 --> 00:02:51,510
And so you could then easily print out uh, month 12, month 11, etc. by referencing the right item

51
00:02:51,510 --> 00:02:52,350
in the tuple.

52
00:02:52,630 --> 00:02:54,430
Okay, so that was tuples.

53
00:02:54,430 --> 00:02:56,670
And they are solid lists.

54
00:02:56,830 --> 00:02:57,950
Solid lists.

